From 13427877d0ebe35501ad078fb3227c4dd58f3626 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: b1nc0d3x <b1nc0d3x@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:10:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] net80211: fullmac: keep vap hooks per vap and put them
 back on detach

The framework saved a vap's iv_newstate, iv_key_set, iv_key_delete and
iv_reset in the per-com state, from the first vap only, and never put
them back.  A station and an access point have different iv_newstate,
so a hostap vap created after a station one ran sta_newstate.  And
ieee80211_fmac_detach, which comes before ieee80211_ifdetach, freed
the state while every vap still pointed at the shims: destroying the
vaps then sent INIT through a shim that could no longer find its
state, returned EINVAL, and tripped the INIT assertion in
ieee80211_newstate_cb.

The hooks are now saved per vap, on a list under fmac_mtx.  The shims
fall back to them when the state is gone, and detach restores every
vap's hooks, ic_vap_delete included, before it frees anything.

A new ic_vap_delete wrapper marks the vap as going and drains the AP
and age tasks before the driver deletes it.  Those tasks walked
ic_vaps without a lock and could create a node on a vap that
ieee80211_node_vdetach had already swept.  They now walk it under the
com lock and pass over a vap being deleted.

Detach drained the age callout before the AP task, which could start
it again on freed state.  The AP task is drained first now, and a
dying flag stops both from arming the callout.  If references do not
drain, detach keeps waiting instead of leaking the state with the
shims still installed, since the caller frees ic right after.

The AP security check read iv_appie_wpa without the com lock, under
which the ioctl path swaps and frees it, and a firmware report for the
vap's own address was joined as a station.
